<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Wix xmlns="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs">
  <!--
    The Windows installer bundle for Drive (drive issue #154).

    What this bundle is for: `winget install Drive` installs one thing, and a
    person who runs it never types a command by hand. winget's WinFsp
    dependency (`Dependencies: PackageDependencies: WinFsp.WinFsp`, in the
    winget manifests beside this file) covers the winget route; this bundle
    covers the same machine when the installer is downloaded directly.

    What it chains:
      1. WinFsp's own official signed MSI, downloaded from WinFsp's own GitHub
         release at build time (see the URL below, pinned to a release tag).
         Nothing of WinFsp's is vendored or copied into this repository.
      2. The Drive MSI (installer/drive.wxs), built by the same CI job.

    WinFsp's licence, and why this route is compliant: WinFsp is GPLv3 with a
    FLOSS exception. The exception's own text permits distributing WinFsp
    "in combination with" a FLOSS application under a GPL-compatible licence.
    Drive is GPLv3-compatible (GPLv3), so the FLOSS exception applies to this
    chain and no commercial licence is needed. No commercial licence is bought
    here, so nothing in this repository spends money. The second WinFsp licence
    (a paid commercial one, for closed-source products) is the route we do NOT
    use, because Drive's source is open under GPLv3.

    Burn is WiX's own bundle engine and part of the same toolchain the MSI is
    built with, so this is one stock toolchain, not a second one.
  -->
  <Bundle
    Name="Drive Setup"
    Manufacturer="Nishfleet"
    Version="1.0.0"
    UpgradeCode="A1B2C3D4-E5F6-7890-ABCD-EF1234567891"
    Compressed="yes">

    <Chain>
      <!--
        WinFsp: the driver that turns an rclone mount into a Windows drive
        letter. Downloaded by the workflow from WinFsp's own release, never
        vendored, and installed with Windows Installer's own silent switch.

        Compressed="no" keeps WinFsp's MSI as a payload that the bundle
        downloads at install time rather than embedding a second vendor's
        binary in ours: this repository carries the URL, not the file.
      -->
      <MsiPackage
        Id="WinFsp"
        SourceFile="winfsp.msi"
        DownloadUrl="https://github.com/winfsp/winfsp/releases/download/v2.1/winfsp-2.1.25156.msi"
        Vital="yes"
        Permanent="no"
        Compressed="no" />

      <!-- Drive itself. The MSI is built beside it by the same CI job from
           installer/drive.wxs. -->
      <MsiPackage Id="Drive" SourceFile="drive.msi" Vital="yes" Compressed="yes" />
    </Chain>
  </Bundle>
</Wix>